{
  "evidence": "TEXT_C: maintain the Human Rights Act and ECHR; support continued EU membership with a double‑majority; reintroduce a post‑study work visa and review immigration detention; appoint an LGBTI rights envoy; oppose blanket data retention; impose a fracking moratorium and prioritise renewables; oppose renewal of the UK nuclear deterrent. TEXT_D: leave the EU and restore UK tax sovereignty; end freedom of movement with an Australian‑style points system, moratorium on unskilled migration, and visa caps; five‑year qualifying period for benefits and most NHS access; increase border enforcement and deport foreign offenders; confirm a UK Bill of Rights and withdraw from EU justice measures; retain the nuclear deterrent and spend at least 2% of GDP on defence; repeal the Climate Change Act, end new wind/solar subsidies; reduce overseas aid.",
  "reasoning": "TEXT_D argues from TAN commitments—national sovereignty over supranational authority, restrictive immigration prioritising order and identity, strong defence and law‑and‑order, and scepticism toward environmental and aid internationalism. TEXT_C argues from GAL commitments—civil liberties, supranational rights and EU integration, multicultural and LGBTI rights, environmental protection, and disarmament—so a more TAN electorate aligns more closely with TEXT_D.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_D"
}
